<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<office:document xmlns:office="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:office:1.0" xmlns:text="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:text:1.0" office:version="1.4" office:mimetype="application/vnd.oasis.opendocument.text" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:style="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:style:1.0" xmlns:fo="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:xsl-fo-compatible:1.0" xmlns:draw="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:drawing:1.0" xmlns:svg="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:svg-compatible:1.0">
 
 <office:automatic-styles>
  <style:style style:name="T1" style:family="text">
   <style:text-properties fo:font-family="monospace"/>
  </style:style>
  <style:style style:name="T2" style:family="text">
   <style:text-properties fo:font-style="italic"/>
  </style:style>
 </office:automatic-styles><office:body>
  <office:text>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">grind</text:h>
    <text:p>grind is an office suite written from scratch. It is 100% written using AI agents. Uses OpenDocument (odf) as its native format.</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="170.000mm" svg:height="113.223mm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="170.000mm" svg:height="128.822mm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">Why this project exists?</text:h>
    <text:p>I&apos;m a software developer and a long-time Linux user. I love and appreciate the free and open source software ecosystem, and I&apos;ve made an effort not to be trapped in non-free file formats for a long time.</text:p>
    <text:p>But I&apos;ve also gotten frustrated with the LibreOffice user experience. Don&apos;t get me wrong, the project has done so much for the free desktop, and it is great software that solves real problems. But the UX shows its age, and the community overall seems not to welcome change. I can never remember how to make Excel-like tables in Calc, and that is not my fault, this is a common operation that should be simple, but Calc makes it hard for some reason.</text:p>
    <text:p>I know, many people will close this repo once they discover it is &apos;slop&apos;. I don&apos;t want to argue if AI agents can possibly be used in ethical ways, but this human written readme should tell you something: This is a passion project. I would not have the time and energy to do this in any different way, an office suite is just way too complex for that.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">Project Status</text:h>
    <text:p>This project has been in development for a few months now and the author is using it on a regular basis. I consider it quite mature at this point in time. I&apos;d love to hear some feedback.</text:p>
    <text:p>Download the <text:a xlink:href="https://github.com/fwilhe2/grind/releases/latest">latest release from github</text:a>.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">Approach</text:h>
    <text:p>This project has clients for Linux, Windows, Mac, the Browser (using WebAssembly), a TUI and a CLI. It does purposefully not use any cross-platform toolkit, it exists as a proof of an idea: AI Agents enable a new way of building software, native for each platform. This was possible before but almost no one did it for economic reasons. Not enough benefit for the cost. With AI Agents, this calculation changes.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">Human and AI Authorship</text:h>
    <text:p>This readme is fully human written. Source code, docs in the <text:span text:style-name="T1">docs</text:span><text:s/>directory and all other content are AI authored.</text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="1">Novel Features</text:h>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">1. View source</text:h>
    <text:p>In spreadsheets, it is often not obvious which cells contain data and which contain code (formulas). Authors may decide to use styles to signal this, but that is not more than a convention. Grind has a plain-text projection of its OpenDocument format which make it simple to review and audit spreadsheets.</text:p>
    <text:p>Here is an example:</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">grind spreadsheet</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// A spreadsheet written by hand — the starter `grind init --template sheet` writes.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">//</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// This file *is* the spreadsheet, in the form that lives in git: every cell is a line, so a</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// change is a line of diff. Open it in grind&apos;s window, or turn it into a file LibreOffice opens.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// A name makes a formula say what it means. Names are the document&apos;s, so they sit outside</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// every sheet.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">name monthly &quot;[$Costs.$B$2:.$B$4]&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">sheet Costs {</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>col 1 width=&quot;4cm&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>// `at` says where a grid of plain values starts; each `row` is one line of the sheet.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>at A1 {</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="8"/>row Item <text:s text:c="6"/>Monthly <text:s/>Yearly</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="8"/>row Rent <text:s text:c="6"/>1450 <text:s text:c="4"/>#null</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="8"/>row Utilities <text:s/>180.5 <text:s text:c="3"/>#null</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="8"/>row Internet <text:s text:c="2"/>49.99 <text:s text:c="3"/>#null</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>}</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>// A `cell` is one cell that carries more than a value — here a formula, and no answer.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>cell C2 &quot;=[.B2]*12&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>cell C3 &quot;=[.B3]*12&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>cell C4 &quot;=[.B4]*12&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>cell A5 Total</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>cell B5 &quot;=SUM(monthly)&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>cell C5 &quot;=SUM([.C2:.C4])&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>style A1:C1 bold=#true background=silver</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>style A5:C5 bold=#true</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="4"/>format B2:C5 currency decimals=2 symbol=&quot;$&quot;</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">}</text:span></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">2. Formulas in plain English</text:h>
    <text:p>Grind can show spreadsheet formulas in a way that is more self documenting compared to other spreadsheet software.</text:p>
    <text:p>So, instead of <text:span text:style-name="T1">=FV(Loan.B7/12;10*12;-Loan.B6)</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p>grind will show you this:</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">Future Value(</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="2"/>Rate: Loan.B7/12,</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="2"/>Number Of Periods: 10*12,</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:s text:c="2"/>Payment: -Loan.B6</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">)</text:span></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">3. A domain specific language to generate documents</text:h>
    <text:p>Based on <text:a xlink:href="https://rhai.rs/">rhai</text:a>, an embedded scripting language you can generate spreadsheets based on dynamic data without the need for a Turing complete language. Grind does not have any macro support and never will, but many macro use-cases might be replaced with rhai scripts.</text:p>
    <text:p>Example:</text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// A script is a sequence of statements, and **its value is the value of its last expression**.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// That value has to be a document, which is why the last line is a bare `s`: returning a sheet</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// means a one-sheet spreadsheet.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">let s = sheet(&quot;Sales&quot;);</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// A header is a row that is bold. `push` takes a bare array or a `row(…)`; the second is what</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// you want when the row needs an adjective.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">s.push(row([&quot;Region&quot;, &quot;Q1&quot;, &quot;Q2&quot;]).bold());</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">s.push([&quot;North&quot;, 4200, 4800]);</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">s.push([&quot;South&quot;, 3100, 3300]);</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// `sum_above()` is told nothing: it sums the contiguous run of numbers directly above wherever</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">// it lands, which is an AutoSum&apos;s rule and the only one that does not reach up into a header.</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">s.push(row([&quot;Total&quot;, sum_above(), sum_above()]).bold());</text:span></text:p>
    <text:p/>
    <text:p><text:span text:style-name="T1">s</text:span></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">4. A Scratch Calculator</text:h>
    <text:p>For the times where you start with a &apos;calculator&apos; app first, but notice a spreadsheet would have been easier to work with later. Define variables, use familiar functions, edit your calculations, promote them to a <text:span text:style-name="T2">real</text:span><text:s/>spreadsheet later.</text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="170.000mm" svg:height="110.870mm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:p><draw:frame text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="170.000mm" svg:height="110.870mm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png"><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">5. A CLI that can do everything</text:h>
    <text:p>The cli is a first class citizen, no matter if it is used by a human, in shell scripts or by AI Agents.</text:p>
    <text:p>Discover what it can do using <text:span text:style-name="T1">grind --help</text:span></text:p>
    <text:h text:outline-level="2">And more</text:h>
    <text:p>Check out the (ai authored) <text:a xlink:href="doc/user-guide.md">user guide for more features</text:a>.</text:p>
